#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# function_convexity

#% text
# `function_convexity(f, x)` gives `1` when `f` is convex on its domain, `-1` when concave, `0` when linear, and `indeterminate` otherwise.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# It is the sign of the second derivative on the domain of `f` (see `function_sign`).

#% item
# `function_convexity([f, cond], x)` restricts the domain.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A parabola and a cubic:

#% cell
function_convexity(x^2, x)
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
function_convexity(x^3, x)
#% output
#= indeterminate

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The logarithm is concave:

#% cell
function_convexity(log(x), x)
#% output
#= -1

#% text
# A linear function:

#% cell
function_convexity(3*x + 1, x)
#% output
#= 0

#% text
# A cubic on half the line:

#% cell
function_convexity([x^3, x > 0], x)
#% output
#= 1

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The sign of the second derivative:

#% cell
function_sign(d(exp(x), [x, 2]), x)
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
function_convexity(exp(x), x)
#% output
#= 1

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `function_sign`, `function_monotonicity`, `d`
